# Svelte 5 Reactivity Traps

Cursor: Svelte 5's runes-based reactivity has eight common traps that LLMs trained on Svelte 4 (or the early `next` previews) consistently hit. Target: `svelte ^5.55.7`.

The companion `sveltekit-anti-patterns` rule rejects the exact code shapes (entries #9, #10, #11, #12, #13, #14, #15, #38).

## Trap 1: `$effect` runs only in the browser

`$effect` is a browser-side mechanism. It does not execute during SSR. State you mutate inside it is `undefined` in the SSR HTML, which produces a hydration mismatch when the effect runs after hydration.

If a value must be present in the SSR markup, compute it in `+page.ts` / `+page.server.ts` `load` and pass it down as a prop.

```svelte
<!-- BAD: empty in SSR HTML, then flashes in after hydration -->
<script lang="ts">
  let now = $state('')
  $effect(() => { now = new Date().toISOString() })
</script>
<p>{now}</p>
```

```ts
// GOOD - +page.ts
import type { PageLoad } from './$types'
export const load: PageLoad = async () => ({ now: new Date().toISOString() })
```

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import type { PageData } from './$types'
  let { data }: { data: PageData } = $props()
</script>
<p>{data.now}</p>
```

## Trap 2: reads after `await` inside `$effect` are NOT tracked

`$effect` only tracks state read synchronously before the first `await`. Reads after the await are dead weight.

```svelte
<!-- BAD - effect does not re-run when `other` changes -->
<script lang="ts">
  let id = $state('a')
  let other = $state('x')
  $effect(async () => {
    await fetch(`/api/${id}`)  // tracks id
    console.log(other)         // does NOT track other
  })
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD - read everything you need to track up front -->
<script lang="ts">
  let id = $state('a')
  let other = $state('x')
  $effect(() => {
    const i = id
    const o = other  // tracked synchronously
    void (async () => {
      await fetch(`/api/${i}`)
      console.log(o)
    })()
  })
</script>
```

## Trap 3: `$effect` infinite loop (read + write same `$state`)

```svelte
<!-- BAD - infinite loop -->
<script lang="ts">
  let value = $state(0)
  $effect(() => { value = value + 1 })  // tracks value, writes value, repeats
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD - if you genuinely need a write that does not retrigger -->
<script lang="ts">
  import { untrack } from 'svelte'
  let value = $state(0)
  let trigger = $state(0)
  $effect(() => {
    trigger
    value = untrack(() => value) + 1
  })
</script>
```

## Trap 4: `$effect` for derivation should be `$derived`

If the new value is a pure function of other state, it is a derivation. Effects are for SIDE effects.

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let count = $state(0)
  let doubled = $state(0)
  $effect(() => { doubled = count * 2 })
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let count = $state(0)
  let doubled = $derived(count * 2)
</script>
```

`$derived` is lazy (recomputed on read), pure, and stays in sync without an effect run.

## Trap 5: `$state.raw` for large arrays

`$state` recursively proxies. For 50,000 row tables the proxy overhead dominates. `$state.raw` opts out; mutate by replacement.

```ts
// BAD - 50,000 rows, each row proxied
let rows = $state<Row[]>(loadFiftyThousandRows())
```

```ts
// GOOD
let rows = $state.raw<Row[]>(loadFiftyThousandRows())
function add(r: Row) { rows = [...rows, r] }
```

Rule of thumb: if you would copy-on-write the value anyway (Redux style), use `$state.raw`.

## Trap 6: destructuring a `$state` proxy captures a snapshot

```ts
// BAD
const cart = $state({ items: [] as Item[], total: 0 })
const { items } = cart
// Reactive consumers reading `items` (the local) re-run only if the local is replaced.
// Mutating `items.push(...)` mutates the proxied array (still reactive for cart.items reads),
// but reading `items` directly in a component template will NOT re-run on cart.items replacement.
```

```ts
// GOOD - read through the proxy
const cart = $state({ items: [] as Item[], total: 0 })
function add(i: Item) { cart.items.push(i) }
// Template: {#each cart.items as item}
```

## Trap 7: mutating a non-`$bindable` prop in the child

Props are read-only in the child by default. Mutating throws in dev. Mark `$bindable()` if you want two-way binding.

```svelte
<!-- BAD - throws in dev -->
<script lang="ts">
  let { value } = $props()
  function reset() { value = '' }
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let { value = $bindable('') }: { value?: string } = $props()
  function reset() { value = '' }
</script>
```

## Trap 8: `bind:this` to a non-`$state` variable

`bind:this` writes to its target after mount. In runes mode, the target must be `$state` for consumers to re-run on the assignment.

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let el: HTMLDivElement
  $effect(() => { console.log(el?.offsetWidth) })  // never sees el
</script>
<div bind:this={el}>...</div>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let el = $state<HTMLDivElement | undefined>()
  $effect(() => { if (el) console.log(el.offsetWidth) })
</script>
<div bind:this={el}>...</div>
```

## Quick decision tree

| Need                                                | Use                       |
| --------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Component-local reactive value                      | `$state`                  |
| Pure derivation of other state                      | `$derived`                |
| Browser-side side effect (DOM, timer, fetch)        | `$effect`                 |
| Same as `$effect` but BEFORE DOM update             | `$effect.pre`             |
| Two-way bindable child prop                         | `$bindable()`             |
| Large dataset, replacement semantics                | `$state.raw`              |
| Read state without tracking                         | `untrack(() => state)`    |
| Plain (non-proxy) snapshot of reactive state        | `$state.snapshot(state)`  |
| SSR-visible value                                   | `load` in `+page.ts`      |
